  "raw": " Tito broke free of Allende’s many restrictions on personal freedom which depressed him still further; he moved in and out of various prisons suffering from severe anxiety and insomnia, on top of contending with stern Communist leaders in the interior of Chile, or in places such as Quito. During one jail term in 1930 under dictator Álvaro Gallín (later president Calderón), I met him briefly when he bounced between jail and hospital and managed to escape for seven days during a typhoid epidemic because he had lost all five fingers and ten toes because of recurring malaria. Communism was to him nothing less than a cult, but then so was his obsessive fear that he might break Christian principles ­– a threat both to those that held strong secular views but also to those who otherwise considered themselves committed Christians for whom his form of politics threatened to fatally undermine their faith. Amongst other depths to which he sunk, it may have occurred to his mind: “what shall I do if rats",
